  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM EST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\n- Rain Tonight, Mixing with Snow Friday Morning\n\nThe going forecast remains on track, as the latest data from this \nmorning continues to support it. A nice soaking rain remains in \norder for later today as rain moves up from South to North through \nthis evening with warm and moist air advection ongoing. The dry \nlower levels will delay the onset as expected, but will be overcome \nas the system moves closer this evening.\n\nThe potential for some light slushy snow accumulations remains \nlikely for very late tonight through Friday morning, before the \nprecipitation ends as rain early Friday afternoon. There remain \nplenty of factors that will limit the accumulation potential with \nthis system. Above freezing layer at the surface, ground temps near \n40F, and a lack of sufficiently cold air aloft (-2 to -3C) all will \nlimit impactful accumulation potential. In addition, snow to liquid \nratios with this thermal profile will only be 6-7:1 most of the \ntime, and best omega is not co-located with the DGZ.\n\nThe best chance for snow late tonight and Friday morning comes with \nlimited solar radiation, some mid level fgen that traverses the area \nthat will help with pcpn rates and snow reaching the ground before \ncompletely melting, which will bring down sfc temps under the better \nrates. When all is said and done, the most likely scenario is areas \nSE of Grand Rapids could see some light slushy accums in grassy \nareas. Can't rule out a little more under isolated heavier bands, \nand some areas see less outside of the bands.\n\nThe coldest air aloft moves out by afternoon, and will change all \nprecipitation back over to rain before ending early Friday afternoon \nfrom West to East.\n\n- Another wave of mainly rain Sunday, Mixing with Snow Late\n\nWe see a brief break in precipitation chances late Friday and Friday \nnight , before some smaller rain chances move through on Saturday. \nThis is associated with a weaker short wave aloft that is centered \nnorth of the area. Most of any moisture will be gone, but there \nmight be just enough to squeeze out sprinkles or a light shower, \nfocused further north of I-96.\n\nThe next better coverage of rain will not take long to arrive, as it \nwill do so on Sunday. Another one of the stronger El Nino southern \nstream systems will emerge out from the Central Plains and lift NE \ntoward the area. This will bring more warm and moist air up from the \nGulf, and bring another soaking rain to the drought affected area \nhere.\n\nAs has been the case for a few days now, a northern stream system \nwill be coming in shortly after the southern stream system rotates \nin. There is a high likelihood that p-types will stay mainly rain, \nas the cold enough air has been consistently shown by the consensus \nof models to come in just a tad too late to phase enough to change \nit to snow. The best chance for some light snow to mix in will be at \nthe tail end of the event Sunday evening, after sunset, and when \nsomewhat cooler air will arrive before the precipitation ends.\n\n- Mainly Dry Monday through Wednesday, and Rain Possible Thursday \n\nThe area has a chance to dry out for Monday through Wednesday after \nthe wet period this Holiday weekend. There is a long wave ridge that \nbuilds over the area, although it is fairly flat in nature. The main \nthing is that it keeps and northern stream short wave activity well \nnorth of the area, and the southern stream is reloading another \nstrong system off of the California coast. This system is poised to \nmove in over the area around the next Thursday time frame. We would \nnot be surprised if an amplified system like this takes a little \nlonger to make it here.",
